 116:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h **** /*
 117:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * Convert a slice holding a JSON number token into a double.
 118:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  *
 119:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * This is the reader-side counterpart to `json_write_double()`: a value
 120:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * written by that function and parsed back out reproduces the original double
 121:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * bit-for-bit. Pass the slice returned by `json_get()` or one of the
 122:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * iterators; the slice is not required to be NUL-terminated.
 123:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  *
 124:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * Returns false if `out` is NULL, if the slice is not a syntactically valid
 125:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * RFC 8259 number, if the token is longer than `JSON_DOUBLE_MAX_TOKEN` bytes,
 126:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * or if the value overflows the range of a double. Values too small to
 127:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * represent underflow to zero and are accepted. `*out` is only written on
 128:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * success.
 129:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  *
 130:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * Note that `json_get()` returns string values without their surrounding
 131:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * quotes, so the JSON value `"1.5"` and the JSON value `1.5` yield identical
 132:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * slices and both convert successfully. Callers that must distinguish a
 133:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * quoted number from a bare one have to inspect the raw document.
 134:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  *
 135:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * Requires `strtod()`, so this function is unavailable in `JSON_NO_STDIO`
 136:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  * builds.
 137:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h ****  */
 138:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h **** bool json_slice_to_double(JsonSlice slice, double *out);
 139:../../ws_rubetec/libjson.h **** 
